  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThe backdoor front that moved through yesterday will lift \nnorthward as a warm front today as a strong area of low \npressure over Hudson Bay moves into Quebec. The warm front will\nprovide a focus for clouds today with mostly cloudy skies across\nmuch of the area by this morning. As deeper moisture arrives \nthere will be chances for isolated showers while the bulk of the\nshower activity arrives with the approaching cold front this \nevening through tonight.\n\nShowers and thunderstorms will form along and ahead of the cold\nfront across the eastern Great Lakes into New York late this\nafternoon. Instability will be waning as thunderstorms move\nacross Vermont and approach the CT Valley after sunset. A few\nrumbles of thunder remain possible tonight while most areas will\nsee 4 to 6 hour window of showers overnight. High PWATs may\nallow periods of moderate rainfall while overall rainfall\namounts will be light with many areas likely only picking up a\ntenth of an inch. Showers will end NW to SE going into Thursday\nmorning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nWe could see a few lingering showers Thursday morning but this \nactivity will be brief as high pressure settles in behind a pair of \ncold fronts.\n\nWesterly winds will already be breezy Thursday morning but will turn \nfairly gusty in the afternoon behind the secondary cold front after \nit sweeps through. Strong cold air advection, steep low-level lapse \nrates, and decent 3-hour pressure rises (2 to 4 mb) will provide \nfavorable conditions for gusty winds to mix their way down to the \nsurface. The NAM still seems to be a bit of a stronger outlier with \nregards to winds at the top of the mixed layer, but widespread gusts \nin the 25 to 35 mph seem reasonable with some isolated gusts up to \n40 mph.\n\nHigh temperatures on Thursday will be warmer for those ahead of the \nsecondary front with some low to mid 80s possible across the \nsouthern half of New Hampshire and interior/coastal Maine. Thursday \nnight will see some cooler lows behind the front, but lingering \nbreezy winds and cloud cover will keep temperatures from bottoming \nout too much. Forecast lows range from the mid to upper 40s north, \nto the mid to upper 50s south and along the coast.\n\nNot much has changed with regards to Friday's forecast and beyond: \ncooler, dry, and breezy with northwest winds gusting up to 25 mph \nthrough the first part of the day. Highs will mainly range from the \nlow to mid 60s north, to the low to mid 70s south. Winds then relax \nunder a clear sky Friday night which will lead to favorable \nradiational cooling conditions. Expect widespread lows in the low to \nmid 40s with some mid to upper 30s possible in the northern valleys.\n\nThe next trough and front will approach on Sunday or Monday, leading \nto our next chances of precipitation which appears to be low impact \nat this time. Expect highs mainly in the 70s through the weekend and \ninto the next work week with a couple of lower 80s across far \nsouthern New Hampshire.",
